{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1385564-post-ragnarok-\u00e6sir","id":1385564,"title":"Post-Ragnarok \u00c6sir","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-04T02:14:57-07:00","id":1385564,"title":"Post-Ragnarok \u00c6sir","content":"Just a heads up, this situation takes inspiration from the 1e book Scion: Ragnarok. As the title suggests, it is about Post-Ragnarok \u00c6sir as well as other characters in a post-Ragnarok setting. So let's get started.<br /> <br /> The New \u00c6sir, Major<br /> Baldr: risen to become the new All-Father.<br /> Fenrir: the new god of wolves.<br /> Hel: Loki's half-dead daughter is Baldr's new wife &amp; rules as queen of the \u00c6sir.<br /> Hod: the blind god of darkness &amp; winter has become the new lord of Helheim.<br /> Magni: god of strength.<br /> Modi: berserker god.<br /> Odin/&quot;Vidar&quot;: Odin survived in the body of his son Vidar &amp; has become a dark rot at the heart of the new \u00c6sir. He seeks to once again be the All-Father.<br /> Tuisco: former Titan avatar &amp; the new god of justice. Less of a warrior than his predecessor, but makes up for it with power over the skies.<br /> <br /> The New \u00c6sir, Minor<br /> Donner: God of Lightning. Donner &amp; Slaegr have managed to forge a new friendship with each other as a result of the situation.<br /> Hermod: God of Couriers.<br /> Slaegr: God of Cunning.<br /> &quot;Tyrone Norris&quot;: God of Honour.<br /> Vali: Goddess of Assassins.<br /> <br /> Others<br /> Angrboda: mother of Fenrir. Has gotten the title Mother of Monsters in this new world.<br /> Elli: goddess of old age &amp; Utgard-Loki's adoptive mother.<br /> The Erlking: an old monster of troll blood.<br /> Jord: Titan avatar of the world.<br /> R\u00e1n: Titan avatar of water.<br /> Thokk: formerly called Nanna, she survived Ragnarok &amp; has a bone to pick with the new king &amp; queen of Asgard.<br /> Utgard-Loki: the Jotun King.<br /> <br /> The Deceased<br /> Freyja: killed by Sinmara, Surtr's sister-daughter.<br /> Freyr: killed by Surtr.<br /> Heimdallr: mutual kill with Loki.<br /> Jormungandr: mutual kill with Thor.<br /> Loki: mutual kill with Heimdallr.<br /> Surtr: killed during Ragnarok.<br /> Thor: mutual kill with Jormungandr.<br /> Tyr: killed by Garm.<br /> <br /> Other Pantheons<br /> So what happened with the other pantheons that feature in the Scion setting? My headcannon says that once Ragnarok was locked in all the stuff from other mythologies (other pantheons, non-Nordic Titan avatars, scions, magical creatures, titanspawn, etc.), all that sort of went dormant, only waking up again when Ragnarok had run its course.<br /> Alternatively, you could say that Ragnarok shook things up &amp; that the stuff from other mythologies existing in the Scion setting is a direct result of the fallout of Ragnarok. Therefore, it can't appear until Ragnarok has run it's course.<br /> <br /> So, anyone else got any ideas for situations like this? Where the structure of a pantheon &amp; all the associated stuff get shaken up? Please share.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26055-krat05","name":"Krat05","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T04:58:48-07:00","id":1385585,"title":"","content":"I love your ideas on post-Ragnarok Aesir, but my personal theory is that Ragnarok does not affect the other pantheons at all. Ragnarok is the end of the Aesir pantheon, not the end of the World. The World has an objective existence beyond the individual pantheons.<br /> <br /> Donner is just the German name for Thor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T05:06:27-07:00","id":1385586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1385564-post-ragnarok-\u00e6sir?p=1385585#post1385585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Donner is just the German name for Thor.</div> </div> </div> </div>The books feature a scion of Thor by the name of Eric Donner. When he ascends to godhood he gets given the &quot;god name&quot; of Donner. So Thor &amp; Donner here are two seperate people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26055-krat05","name":"Krat05","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T05:10:24-07:00","id":1385587,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1385564-post-ragnarok-\u00e6sir?p=1385585#post1385585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love your ideas on post-Ragnarok Aesir, but my personal theory is that Ragnarok does not affect the other pantheons at all. Ragnarok is the end of the Aesir pantheon, not the end of the World. The World has an objective existence beyond the individual pantheons.</div> </div> </div> </div>May I again point out that I built this new \u00c6sir pantheon from the various events of Ragnarok featured in the 1e sourcebook Scion: Ragnarok which end up being pretty apocalyptic for the world at large. The other pantheons are absolutely going to be affected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26055-krat05","name":"Krat05","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T05:14:40-07:00","id":1385588,"title":"","content":"Okay. I never read that. But I think they changed that in 2e to make the pantheons more independent of each other.<br /> <br /> Also, some people think Ragnarok already happened around the twelfth century when Scandinavia became Christian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-04T05:17:04-07:00","id":1385590,"title":"","content":"Again, I never read the 1e books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]}]}